<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arrian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 86–c. 160</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Arrian of Nicomedia, Lucius Flavius Arrianus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arrian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> was a Greek historian, philosopher and Roman official of the second century CE. A pupil of Epictetus, whose teachings he recorded in the Discourses and the Enchiridion, he governed Cappadocia under Hadrian and repelled an invasion by the Alans around 135. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabasis">Anabasis</a> of Alexander is the most important surviving account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>'s campaigns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arrian.jpg" alt="Arrian" width="220" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WikiSysop · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arrian7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arrian was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a>, the provincial capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>, between about 85 and 90 CE. His full Roman name, Lucius Flavius Arrianus, shows that he was a Roman citizen, and his family belonged to the Greek provincial aristocracy, with citizenship probably going back several generations to the time of the Roman conquest. While in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, probably at Nicopolis, he attended the lectures of Epictetus and became his pupil. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> he came to be known as the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>, because his relationship to Epictetus resembled Xenophon's to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>About 126 he was a friend of the emperor Hadrian, who appointed him to the Senate. He became consul suffectus about 130 and in 132, though one scholar gives 131, Hadrian made him governor of Cappadocia, a post he held for six years. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba">Bar Kokhba</a> revolt was suppressed in 135, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharasmanes-ii-of-iberia">Pharasmanes II of Iberia</a> caused the Alani to invade neighbouring lands, including Cappadocia, where Arrian's legions halted them. Dio adds that the Alani were also persuaded by gifts from Vologaesus and stood in dread of Arrian. Lucian called him a Roman of the first rank with a life-long attachment to learning, and Arrian himself used the title of second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Eight of his works survive; only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabasis">Anabasis</a> and the Indica are wholly intact. The Anabasis of Alexander, in seven books, is considered the best source on the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>'s account of the march of Cyrus. Scholars have generally preferred Arrian to other surviving primary sources, though modern study of his method has qualified this. The Indica draws on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and on the journal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nearchus">Nearchus</a> for the voyage in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>.</p><p>He recorded the teaching of Epictetus in the Discourses, which he says he published because his notes were being circulated without authority, and in the Enchiridion, a short handbook of Epictetus's principles. Other works include a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus</a> of the Euxine Sea addressed to Hadrian, the Techne Taktike on cavalry and tactics (136 or 137), and a treatise on hunting with hounds meant as an addition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>. His Parthica, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s Parthian war, his history of Alexander's successors and several biographies are lost or survive in fragments. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a> addressed seven letters to him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arrian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 135 another Alan invasion struck Albania, Media and Armenia; <b>Flavius Arrian</b>, governor of Cappadocia, prepared his army to meet them and wrote a short treatise, the Order of Battle against the Alans, describing how Roman infantry should receive the charge of heavy cavalry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Arrian</b>, sent by Hadrian, found the Laz in control of most of coastal Colchis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-colchis">Kingdom of Colchis · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>According to <b>Arrian</b>, the Iberians sent envoys to Alexander the Great in 324 BCE, along with embassies of Carthaginians, Italics and Gauls, to ask for his friendship.</blockquote><a href="/culture/iberians">Iberians · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Arrian</b>, touring the coast for Emperor Hadrian in the 130s, recorded a Lazi king holding power by Roman appointment, and the Lazi gradually united neighbouring tribes.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lazica">Kingdom of Lazica</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Arrian</b> reports that the wounded Porus, asked how he wished to be treated, answered: like a king.</blockquote><a href="/state/paurava-kingdom">Paurava kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.76,29.92&amp;year=134" aria-label="Nicomedia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5283 708 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arrian"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5733 978h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5733 978h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.76,29.92&amp;year=134">What was here in 134 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In relating the history of Alexander’s achievements, there are some things which I have been compelled to censure; but I am not ashamed to admire Alexander himself.&#8221;</p><footer>Anabasis of Alexander, book 7 &#183; tr. E. J. Chinnock, 1884 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46976/pg46976-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
